\n1187 | \n1188 |         A calibration curve, also known as a reliability diagram, uses inputs\n1189 |         from a binary classifier and plots the average predicted probability\n1190 |         for each bin against the fraction of positive classes, on the\n1191 |         y-axis.\n1192 | \n1193 |         Extra keyword arguments will be passed to\n1194 |         :func:`matplotlib.pyplot.plot`.\n1195 | \n1196 |         Read more about calibration in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>` and\n1197 |         more about the scikit-learn visualization API in :ref:`visualizations`.\n1198 | \n1199 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1200 | \n1201 |         Parameters\n1202 |         ----------\n1203 |         estimator : estimator instance\n1204 |             Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n1205 |             in which the last estimator is a classifier. The classifier must\n1206 |             have a :term:`predict_proba` method.\n1207 | \n1208 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1209 |             Input values.\n1210 | \n1211 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n1212 |             Binary target values.\n1213 | \n1214 |         n_bins : int, default=5\n1215 |             Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval into when\n1216 |             calculating the calibration curve. A bigger number requires more\n1217 |             data.\n1218 | \n1219 |         strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n1220 |             Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n1221 | \n1222 |             - `'uniform'`: The bins have identical widths.\n1223 |             - `'quantile'`: The bins have the same number of samples and depend\n1224 |               on predicted probabilities.\n1225 | \n1226 |         pos_label : int, float, bool or str, default=None\n1227 |             The positive class when computing the calibration curve.\n1228 |             By default, `estimators.classes_[