X, value_to_mask):\n39 |     \"\"\"Compute the boolean mask X == value_to_mask.\n40 | \n41 |     Parameters\n42 |     ----------\n43 |     X : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n44 |         Input data, where ``n_samples`` is the number of samples and\n45 |         ``n_features`` is the number of features.\n46 | \n47 |     value_to_mask : {int, float}\n48 |         The value which is to be masked in X.\n49 | \n50 |     Returns\n51 |     -------\n52 |     X_mask : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n53 |         Missing mask.\n54 |     \"\"\"\n55 |     if not sp.issparse(X):\n56 |         # For all cases apart of a sparse input where we need to reconstruct\n57 |         # a sparse output\n58 |         return _get_dense_mask(X, value_to_mask)\n59 | \n60 |     Xt = _get_dense_mask(X.data, value_to_mask)\n61 | \n62 |     sparse_constructor = sp.csr_matrix if X.format == \"csr\" else sp.csc_matrix\n63 |     Xt_sparse = sparse_constructor(\n64 |         (Xt, X.indices.copy(), X.indptr.copy()), shape=X.shape, dtype=bool\n65 |     )\n66 | \n67 |     return Xt_sparse\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me get more details about the core tag system implementation.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/utils/_tags.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/utils/_tags.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/utils/_tags.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/utils/_tags.py`.\n```\n  1 | from __future__ import annotations\n  2 | \n  3 | import warnings\n  4 | from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n  5 | \n  6 | from .fixes import _dataclass_args\n  7 | \n  8 | # Authors: The scikit-learn developers\n  9 | # SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause\n 10 | \n 11 | \n 12 | @dataclass(**_dataclass_args())\n 13 | class InputTags:\n 14 |     \"\"\"Tags for the input data.\n 15 | \n 16 |     Parameters\n 17 |     ----------\n 18 |     one_d_array : bool, default=False\n 19 |         Whether the input can be a 1D array.\n 20 | \n 21 |     two_d_array : bool, default=True\n 22 |         Whether the input can be a 2D array. Note that most common\n 23 |         tests currently run only if this flag is set to ``True``.\n 24 | \n 25 |     three_d_array : bool, default=False\n 26 |         Whether the input can be a 3D array.\n 27 | \n 28 |     sparse : bool, default=False\n 29 |         Whether the input can be a sparse matrix.\n 30 | \n 31 |     categorical : bool, default=False\n 32 |         Whether the input can be categorical.\n 33 | \n 34 |     string : bool, default=False\n 35 |         Whether the input can be an array-like of strings.\n 36 | \n 37 |     dict : bool, default=False\n 38 |         Whether the input can be a dictionary.\n 39 | \n 40 |     positive_only : bool, default=False\n 41 |         Whether the estimator requires positive X.\n 42 | \n 43 |     allow_nan : bool, default=False\n 44 |         Whether the estimator supports data with missing values encoded as `np.nan`.\n 45 | \n 46 |     pairwise : bool, default=False\n 47 |         This boolean attribute indicates whether the data (`X`),\n 48 |         :term:`fit` and similar methods consists of pairwise measures\n 49 |         over samples rather than a feature representation for each\n 50 |         sample.  It is usually `True` where an estimator has a\n 51 |         `metric` or `affinity` or `kernel` parameter with value\n 52 |         'precomputed'. Its primary purpose is to support a\n 53 |         :term:`meta-estimator` or a cross validation procedure that\n 54 |         extracts a sub-sample of data intended for a pairwise\n 55 |         estimator, where the data needs to be indexed on both axes.\n 56 |         Specifically, this tag is used by\n 57 |         `sklearn.utils.metaestimators._safe_split` to